\cf0 Your empathy and humility scores are real assets, but they're baseline traits \'97 they don't automatically activate under stress. The gap to watch isn't "are you empathetic," it's "does your empathy still function when you're rushed, threatened, or winning."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 behavioural risks to monitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Decision-making that outruns consultation.
\f0\b0  Under time pressure, there may be a pull toward making the efficient call rather than the collaborative one. Watch for: finalizing decisions before checking in with people who'll be affected; treating "we don't have time to discuss this" as self-evidently true rather than testing it.\

\f1\b Selective framing under stakes.
\f0\b0  In negotiations, pitches, or reporting upward, there may be a tendency to present information in whatever shape best supports the preferred outcome. Watch for: leaving out inconvenient data points rather than flagging them; noticing you're constructing a "case" rather than a summary.\

\f1\b Credit and attribution drift in competitive moments.
\f0\b0  In visibility-heavy situations (performance reviews, exec updates, promotion cycles), there's a risk of your role in a win becoming the default narrative. Watch for: retelling a team success without naming who did what; feeling irritated rather than glad when someone else gets recognized for shared work.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Top 3 strengths to build on\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 High humility (75)
\f0\b0  is your best regulator. It means you're more likely to actually hear critical feedback instead of deflecting it \'97 which is precisely the mechanism that catches the risks above before they compound. Humility doesn't prevent the instinct to cut corners; it makes you more likely to notice and correct it afterward, or better, in the moment.\

\f1\b Solid empathy (61)
\f0\b0  is a real counterweight to purely instrumental thinking. It's the internal voice that notices "this decision affects someone" even when a strategic calculation says to move fast. The task is protecting it under stress, not building it from scratch.\

\f1\b Ethical leadership (68)
\f0\b0  suggests you already have \'97 or want \'97 some structure (values, principles, a sense of what "good" looks like) that operates independently of momentary incentives. That structure is exactly what holds when in-the-moment judgment is compromised by pressure or ambition.\
Together: humility catches the mistake, empathy flags the harm, ethical leadership gives you a standard to correct toward. The plan below is about strengthening those three as active habits, not just latent traits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 4. 2\'964 week practice plan\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Week 1 \'97 Listen before you weigh in
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  In every meeting, let at least two other people speak before you give your view.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection:
\f0\i0  When did I feel the urge to jump in first? What did I think I'd lose by waiting?\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour:
\f0\i0  At least one instance where someone else's point changed your position, and you said so out loud.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 2 \'97 Make credit explicit
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  Every time you report on a win \'97 up, sideways, or in writing \'97 name the specific person and their specific contribution, not just "the team."\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection:
\f0\i0  Did I feel any resistance to naming someone else's role? What was that resistance protecting?\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour:
\f0\i0  A written or verbal record (email, Slack, meeting) where you named someone's contribution specifically.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 3 \'97 Install a pause before non-urgent decisions
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  For any decision that isn't genuinely time-critical, write down your reasoning, wait 24 hours, then review it before acting.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection:
\f0\i0  What pressure was pushing me toward speed? Did the delay change the decision, or just the way I felt about it?\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour:
\f0\i0  A log of at least 3 decisions where you used the pause, noting whether the outcome shifted.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 4 \'97 Actively solicit and act on feedback
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  Ask 2\'963 colleagues for one specific, recent example of something you could have done differently (see script below). Pick one piece of feedback and visibly act on it.\
